WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.266 --> 00:00:00.834 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-Congratulations.

00:00:00.834 --> 00:00:02.602 align:start size:94% position:6%
This film nominated
for a Golden Globe yesterday.

00:00:02.602 --> 00:00:04.037 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
-Congratulations on that.

00:00:04.037 --> 00:00:06.873 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Thank you.
-And for those who don't know,

00:00:06.873 --> 00:00:08.675 line:92%
this is based on the making

00:00:08.675 --> 00:00:10.577 align:start size:94% position:6%
of an actual film
called "The Room."

00:00:10.577 --> 00:00:11.845 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah, there's a movie
called "The Room,"

00:00:11.845 --> 00:00:16.049 align:start size:94% position:6%
not "Room" with Brie Larson
which is a great movie.

00:00:16.049 --> 00:00:18.118 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah, exactly.
-And Jacob Tremblay

00:00:18.118 --> 00:00:21.287 align:start size:94% position:6%
is darling and emotionally
available.

00:00:21.287 --> 00:00:22.088 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:00:22.088 --> 00:00:24.791 align:start size:94% position:6%
But this is a movie
called "The Room."

00:00:24.791 --> 00:00:28.862 align:start size:94% position:6%
with Tommy Wiseau,
who James Franco plays.

00:00:28.862 --> 00:00:33.033
He wrote it, directed it,
stars in it, produced it,

00:00:33.033 --> 00:00:37.470 size:75% position:38%
financed it himself,
marketed it himself.

00:00:37.470 --> 00:00:41.574
And it is known for being one
of the worst movies of all-time.

00:00:41.574 --> 00:00:42.609 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:00:42.609 --> 00:00:46.179 align:start size:94% position:6%
-And it's so bad,
that it actually developed,

00:00:46.179 --> 00:00:47.113 size:84% position:42% line:92%
like, a cult following.

00:00:47.113 --> 00:00:50.784 size:75% position:38%
And people, like me,
started seeing it.

00:00:50.784 --> 00:00:52.719 align:start size:94% position:6%
with their friends
and telling people to see it,

00:00:52.719 --> 00:00:54.421 align:start size:94% position:6%
And it became kind of like
Rocky Horror

00:00:54.421 --> 00:00:57.257 align:start size:94% position:6%
Picture Show-esque experience
where it was kind of

00:00:57.257 --> 00:00:58.992 size:84% position:42%
interactive with the
audience and they would

00:00:58.992 --> 00:01:04.431
participate in kind of just
the glory of how absolutely,

00:01:04.431 --> 00:01:07.000 line:92%
ridiculously terrible it is

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:08.635
to the point that people started
liking it, though.

00:01:08.635 --> 00:01:10.737 align:start size:94% position:6%
And honestly, like,
I watched it so many times,

00:01:10.737 --> 00:01:11.371 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
I kind of love it,

00:01:11.371 --> 00:01:12.639 align:start size:94% position:6%
and we made
a whole movie about it.

00:01:12.639 --> 00:01:14.674 align:start size:94% position:6%
And there is something great
about it.

00:01:14.674 --> 00:01:17.143 align:start size:94% position:6%
And because
you keep watching it.

00:01:17.143 --> 00:01:18.812 align:start size:94% position:6%
Like, and if it was bad,
you were like,

00:01:18.812 --> 00:01:21.147 align:start size:94% position:6%
"Why am I watching this over
and over again?

00:01:21.147 --> 00:01:24.651
Why have I seen this more than
I've seen, like, good movies?"

00:01:24.651 --> 00:01:28.221 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
-Like -- and so, yeah.

00:01:28.221 --> 00:01:29.622 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's endlessly fascinating
subject.

00:01:29.622 --> 00:01:31.991 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Who introduced it to you?
It came out in 2003.

00:01:31.991 --> 00:01:33.259
Did you see it pretty early on,
do you think?

00:01:33.259 --> 00:01:35.295
-Yeah, I saw it about a year and
a half maybe after it came out.

00:01:35.295 --> 00:01:38.965
Paul Rudd I think was the first
one who mentioned it to me,

00:01:38.965 --> 00:01:39.766 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
and he had seen it.

00:01:39.766 --> 00:01:41.501 line:92%
And then I went and saw it

00:01:41.501 --> 00:01:43.970 align:start size:94% position:6%
with my wife,
who I was dating at the time

00:01:43.970 --> 00:01:45.939 align:start size:94% position:6%
and Jonah Hill
and Ed Helms actually...

00:01:45.939 --> 00:01:49.876 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Seth: Wow.
-At the Sunset Laemmle Five.

00:01:49.876 --> 00:01:51.845 size:88% position:44% line:92%
And it was mostly empty.

00:01:51.845 --> 00:01:54.147 align:start size:94% position:6%
And there was just kind
of people starting

00:01:54.147 --> 00:01:55.148 size:75% position:38% line:92%
to interact with it,

00:01:55.148 --> 00:02:00.120 align:start size:94% position:6%
but it's catastrophically,
bizarrely bad.

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:01.287 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Laughter ]
-I --

00:02:01.287 --> 00:02:02.455 line:92%
so I knew about the movie.

00:02:02.455 --> 00:02:03.923 align:start size:94% position:6%
I hadn't seen it,
but I used to --

00:02:03.923 --> 00:02:06.392 align:start size:94% position:6%
when I would visit L.A.,
would you see this billboard.

00:02:06.392 --> 00:02:09.129 align:start size:94% position:6%
And this billboard
was up for a while.

00:02:09.129 --> 00:02:11.264 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Years, years, and that's
the marketing

00:02:11.264 --> 00:02:15.268 align:start size:94% position:6%
I was referring to.
Tommy paid to keep that up.

00:02:15.268 --> 00:02:17.437 align:start size:94% position:6%
It had a Website --
theroommovie.com

00:02:17.437 --> 00:02:20.473 size:88% position:44%
There was a phone number
and you would call it,

00:02:20.473 --> 00:02:23.042
and you didn't need to,
because you could just go

00:02:23.042 --> 00:02:24.811 line:92%
to the theater and buy tickets.

00:02:24.811 --> 00:02:27.380 align:start size:94% position:6%
And the phone number
was just Tommy's phone number

00:02:27.380 --> 00:02:30.950 align:start size:94% position:6%
and he would just answer
the phone and just say, like,

00:02:30.950 --> 00:02:31.718 line:92%
"Are you coming to movie?"

00:02:31.718 --> 00:02:33.686 align:start size:94% position:6%
And you would be like, "Yeah.
I'm coming to the movie."

00:02:33.686 --> 00:02:36.422 size:84% position:42%
And he would be like,
"Great, see you there."

00:02:36.422 --> 00:02:38.992 align:start size:94% position:6%
And that was the entirety
of the interaction.

00:02:38.992 --> 00:02:41.561 size:78% position:39%
And there was also --
he ran TV commercials

00:02:41.561 --> 00:02:43.663 align:start size:94% position:6%
really late at night
that I had seen a few of.

00:02:43.663 --> 00:02:45.532 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I asked him about it,
and he said,

00:02:45.532 --> 00:02:48.034
"They were on so late at night,
they were free."

00:02:48.034 --> 00:02:49.369 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:02:49.369 --> 00:02:50.637 align:start size:94% position:6%
-like, it was literally
at a time

00:02:50.637 --> 00:02:52.238 align:start size:94% position:6%
when there was just
nothing else.

00:02:52.238 --> 00:02:57.277
So they let him put his
ridiculous ads up for his movie.

00:02:57.277 --> 00:02:58.244 align:start size:94% position:6%
Yeah.
-It's fantastic.

00:02:58.244 --> 00:03:00.780
You were a producer on the film
from the start.

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:01.481 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
-And --

00:03:01.481 --> 00:03:02.549 align:start size:94% position:6%
but then you decide
to play a part.

00:03:02.549 --> 00:03:04.184
We saw the part you were playing
there in the clip.

00:03:04.184 --> 00:03:06.319 align:start size:94% position:6%
How did you decide
to play that character?

00:03:06.319 --> 00:03:09.355 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Oh, he was like the only guy
around my age, I think.

00:03:09.355 --> 00:03:11.291 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Okay.
-[ Laughs ]

00:03:11.291 --> 00:03:12.592 align:start size:94% position:6%
But --
-You're making producing

00:03:12.592 --> 00:03:15.195 align:start size:94% position:6%
sound very easy.
-Well, yeah.

00:03:15.195 --> 00:03:17.297 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I played Sandy Schklair,
is his name

00:03:17.297 --> 00:03:19.399 align:start size:94% position:6%
and he was a script
supervisor of "The Room,"

00:03:19.399 --> 00:03:22.335
and kind of, sort of directed it
a little bit.

00:03:22.335 --> 00:03:24.771 align:start size:94% position:6%
As you can see,
he kind of like calls action

00:03:24.771 --> 00:03:25.572 line:92%
and helps with the lines.

00:03:25.572 --> 00:03:28.641 size:81% position:41%
And he later claimed
he directed the movie,

00:03:28.641 --> 00:03:31.544 align:start size:94% position:6%
which shows
what a weird thing it is,

00:03:31.544 --> 00:03:33.079 align:start size:94% position:6%
because it's a bad movie.
-Yeah.

00:03:33.079 --> 00:03:35.582 align:start size:94% position:6%
-So this guy is taking
credit for it.

00:03:35.582 --> 00:03:37.650 align:start size:94% position:6%
Like, we're suing,
hoarding the credit

00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:41.354
for creating something that is
only famous because it's bad.

00:03:41.354 --> 00:03:44.290 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's so weird.
It's a very weird thing, yeah.

00:03:44.290 --> 00:03:48.061 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Does -- do you think Tommy,
looking back, is happy?

00:03:48.061 --> 00:03:50.129 align:start size:94% position:6%
Obviously, this film
has succeeded to a place

00:03:50.129 --> 00:03:53.866
where it's being adapted
by all these great actors

00:03:53.866 --> 00:03:54.968 align:start size:94% position:6%
and writers and directors.
-Yeah.

00:03:54.968 --> 00:03:56.869 align:start size:94% position:6%
-But it was not what he
set out to make.

00:03:56.869 --> 00:03:59.672 align:start size:94% position:6%
-No, and that is like
an interesting moment to me --

00:03:59.672 --> 00:04:03.743 align:start size:94% position:6%
Is like he did have to kind
of readjust what his goal was.

00:04:03.743 --> 00:04:06.613
He had to pretend his goal
was to make a ridiculous comedy,

00:04:06.613 --> 00:04:10.316
whereas his goal was really
to make like a heartfelt drama.

00:04:10.316 --> 00:04:13.386 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Laughter ]
But he -- but he --

00:04:13.386 --> 00:04:17.690
but he -- but his goal was also
to become a famous movie star,

00:04:17.690 --> 00:04:19.659 align:start size:94% position:6%
which he kind of is now
in some way

00:04:19.659 --> 00:04:22.829
and a famous filmmaker --
which he kind of is now

00:04:22.829 --> 00:04:24.464 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
in some way.

00:04:24.464 --> 00:04:25.932 line:92%
Like, I mean, we had a --

00:04:25.932 --> 00:04:27.333 align:start size:94% position:6%
James Franco plays him
in the movie.

00:04:27.333 --> 00:04:29.836 size:88% position:44%
He wanted Johnny Depp,
but we got James Franco.

00:04:29.836 --> 00:04:31.804 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:04:31.804 --> 00:04:33.706 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
But, yeah.

00:04:33.706 --> 00:04:36.042 align:start size:94% position:6%
But what's weird is like
he seems --

00:04:36.042 --> 00:04:38.311 align:start size:94% position:6%
I was just looking at him
at the premier,

00:04:38.311 --> 00:04:38.945 size:88% position:44% line:92%
and it was a big premier

00:04:38.945 --> 00:04:40.446
and like a lot of famous people
were there.

00:04:40.446 --> 00:04:42.582 align:start size:94% position:6%
A lot of famous people
were at the movie,

00:04:42.582 --> 00:04:45.652 line:92%
And he is behaving in such a way

00:04:45.652 --> 00:04:48.721 align:start size:94% position:6%
as though he always
expected it to happen.

00:04:48.721 --> 00:04:51.724
And, like, I was saying --
my only other experience

00:04:51.724 --> 00:04:53.860 align:start size:94% position:6%
is I played Steve Wozniak
in a movie,

00:04:53.860 --> 00:04:55.962 align:start size:94% position:6%
and like,
that guy invented computers

00:04:55.962 --> 00:04:59.599 align:start size:94% position:6%
and he was blown away we were
making a movie about him.

00:04:59.599 --> 00:05:01.334 line:92%
Like, he couldn't believe it.

00:05:01.334 --> 00:05:04.003 align:start size:94% position:6%
This guy made
the worst movie of all-time

00:05:04.003 --> 00:05:07.040
and it's as though not only
was he expecting a movie

00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:08.574 size:78% position:39%
to be made about him,
he's a little pissed

00:05:08.574 --> 00:05:10.643 align:start size:94% position:6%
it wasn't five
years ago, basically.

00:05:10.643 --> 00:05:11.477 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah
[ Laughter ]

00:05:11.477 --> 00:05:13.813 size:78% position:39%
-And that Johnny Depp
isn't playing him,

00:05:13.813 --> 00:05:15.548 line:92%
and instead James Franco is.

00:05:15.548 --> 00:05:17.950 align:start size:94% position:6%
He's a weird dude.
-You --

00:05:17.950 --> 00:05:20.753 align:start size:94% position:6%
you saw him before
even you began

00:05:20.753 --> 00:05:22.388 align:start size:94% position:6%
to talk about
adapting the story.

00:05:22.388 --> 00:05:23.790 align:start size:94% position:6%
You would see him
in L.A.

00:05:23.790 --> 00:05:25.058 line:92%
You saw him at grocery stores?

00:05:25.058 --> 00:05:25.925 line:92%
I'-d see him at whole foods.

00:05:25.925 --> 00:05:27.794 size:84% position:42%
Me and Jonah, actually,
would shop at the same

00:05:27.794 --> 00:05:29.662 align:start size:94% position:6%
whole foods and he first
saw him there

00:05:29.662 --> 00:05:31.197 align:start size:94% position:6%
and sent me a picture of him
like in front of, like,

00:05:31.197 --> 00:05:34.334 align:start size:94% position:6%
in front of cantaloupes
with Tommy.

00:05:34.334 --> 00:05:35.501 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:05:35.501 --> 00:05:37.236 line:92%
And I was like, "Oh, my God."

00:05:37.236 --> 00:05:39.405 align:start size:94% position:6%
I knew I was like --
and I would keep my eye out --

00:05:39.405 --> 00:05:41.674 align:start size:94% position:6%
open for him,
and then I saw him one time

00:05:41.674 --> 00:05:42.642 line:92%
and I took a picture with him.

00:05:42.642 --> 00:05:45.745
And -- and then years later
when we were making a movie,

00:05:45.745 --> 00:05:48.147 align:start size:94% position:6%
He like instantly,
"You take picture with me

00:05:48.147 --> 00:05:49.782 line:92%
one time at grocery store.

00:05:49.782 --> 00:05:50.683 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:05:50.683 --> 00:05:53.186 align:start size:94% position:6%
You big fan of mine.
You real big fan of mine.

00:05:53.186 --> 00:05:55.722 align:start size:94% position:6%
You like me."
"Yeah, Tommy."

